  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nExpansive upper ridge centered over the Great Basin still\nremains in place. However in the Northern Plains, ridge is\nsuppressed in the wake of a well developed upper level shortwave\ntrough in the mid Mississippi Valley and western Great Lakes.\nThis is finally offering a reprieve from the recent stretch of \nheat and higher humidity the past few weeks as relatively cooler\nand drier air mass infiltrates our region. Surface high pressure\nis also helping keep rain chances minimal and skies mostly\nclear. There is a low chance for patchy fog tonight given clear\nskies and light winds, particularly for areas that received\nrecent rainfall.\n\nThe reprieve in heat is short-lived as upper ridge starts to \nbuild into the Northern Plains this weekend. This will\nreintroduce hot air mass into the Dakotas from the High Plains.\nIt remains to be seen how far this air mass and heat stretches\ninto our area of eastern ND and northwest/west-central MN,\nalthough there is a low chance for heat impacts.\n\nThis also occurs as a seasonably stout upper trough encroaches\nupon the Dakotas and Minnesota Sunday and Monday. This will\nbring the next chance for thunderstorms, some of which may be\nsevere. More details on this found below.\n\nThere is also a signal for near critical or critical fire\nweather conditions Sunday afternoon. This is due to gusty\nsoutherly winds greater than 20 mph, but likely to stay below \n45 mph, highest in eastern ND and Red River Valley. Minimum RH \nvalues are currently forecast in the 30 to 50 percent range, \nhowever, confidence in this is low, with potential to dip into \nthe 25 to 35 percent range. Temperatures increase Sunday as well\ninto the 80s and 90s.\n\n...Severe potential late Sunday and Monday...\n\nWith the approach of a stout upper trough from west to east, \nsynoptic forcing aloft increases as does increasing wind energy.\nThis will also help return low level moisture into our area -\nhowever, the degree of which remains uncertain. Additionally,\nthere a tongue of steep lapse rates aloft overspreading the warm\nsector during this time. As this warm sector noses into the\nregion, elevated robust thunderstorms are expected late Sunday,\nof which may impact our area. Should this occur, storms may be\nsevere with large hail the main hazard. This activity could\nlinger Sunday overnight into Monday morning.\n\nDuring the day Monday, majority of ensemble guidance swings a\ncold front through our area, providing focus for ascent and\nsparking thunderstorms. There is some uncertainty in strength \nof capping, although this doesn't appear to be an \"unbreakable\" \ncap, especially when considering the degree of forcing for \nascent that could erode/break through capping inversion.\n\nWhile kinematic parameter spacing looks to be on the stronger\nside (mean bulk shear within ensembles is around 50kt),\ninstability is a bit more questionable. While antecedent air\nmass ahead of the warm sector is rather devoid of rich moisture,\nthere may be enough return and pooling ahead of the approaching\nfront to allow for at least a narrow corridor of rich low level\nmoisture aiding in instability and overall low level buoyancy.\n\nUltimately, this still allows for the potential of all hazards \nconsidering discrete storm mode still remains possible. AI / ML \nguidance like StormNet supports this. However, orientation of \nthe cold front and upper trough axis may not support discrete \nstorm mode. Thus, storm mode and its influence to dictate \ndominant hazards still remains questionable.",
